WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
We had two distinct families.

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:13.020
We had the Towner side who were all loving and down to earth and just loved everybody.

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.560
They real huggy kissy type.

4
00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.180
Then we had the Perkins side who were a different type.

5
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.380
They were very stanch and upright and everything had to be perfect.

6
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.460
So I guess we had the best of both worlds.

7
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:41.660
And on my grandfather's side, the Towners, I had a great-great-grandfather who learned to read and write at seventy-eight years old.

8
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.820
He had something happen to him.

9
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:52.620
They lived up at Deridder in Sugartown and he bought, during the time when they had the . . . you could go homestead acreage.

10
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:59.480
He homesteaded some acreage up there and he couldn't read or write so he depended on someone to take care of it for him.

11
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.560
And the man ended up taking the land from him.

12
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.280
And Grandpa, they said that he said he would never let that happen again.

13
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.700
So he went to the school . . . At that time it was segregated,

14
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.340
but he went to the black school there and he learned to read and write, so he could know what was going on.

15
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.420
He didn't have to depend on somebody else.

16
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.860
My family, they were big, both sides, were big on education.

17
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.400
Big on education.

18
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:34.600
We thought that besides being a person of your word, if you shook hands that was just like signing your name in blood.

19
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.700
That's the way they were.

20
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.660
But they also realized that you had to have an education so that you could know what was going on.

21
00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.520
You couldn’t . . . You don't have to depend on anybody else to tell you.

22
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.420
Because you can read, you can write.

23
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.780
So they were sticklers on education.
